dependent task, then check your steps.","passage":"Earthworms tunnel through soil, allowing air and water into the ground. They also feed on decaying plant material.","prompts":["Record the key information.","Show the method.","Check the result."]},"reflection_prompts":null,"reflection_prompt":null,"teach_steps":[{"icon":"🔎","title":"Find the topic","explain":"**Why this works:** A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel.\n\n**Remember:** Use the original facts, numbers and positions rather than guessing. This lesson investigates main idea detective: find supporting details.","example":"**MY THINKING:** A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel.\n\n**YOUR TRY:** Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.\n\n**CHECK:** Read the model and explain where each number or supporting detail came from.","notice":"A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel. Keep your work accurate and name the strategy.","check":{"question":"Topic?","options":["Native bees and flowers","Ocean tides","Moon phases","Bicycles"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct choice is Native bees and flowers. A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel."},"visual_before":[{"episode":{"title":"Investigate Main Idea Detective: Find Supporting Details","transcript":"Pip: I found a tricky task and need a reliable method.\nJuno: Look carefully at the example and explain each step.\nPip: I can use the worked example to check my answer.\nJuno: Pause and try solving one part before the next stage.","scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"who":"pip","text":"I found a tricky task and need a reliable method. How do I decide what to do?"},{"who":"juno","text":"This is main idea detective: find supporting details. Look carefully at the example and explain each step."},{"who":"pip","text":"I can use the worked example to check my answer and explain my method."},{"who":"juno","text":"Pause and try solving one part before the next stage. Check it carefully, including the original numbers or supporting words.","pause_after":true}]}]}}]},{"icon":"📚","title":"State the main idea","explain":"**Why this works:** If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea.\n\n**Remember:** Use the original facts, numbers and positions rather than guessing. This lesson investigates main idea detective: find supporting details.","example":"**MY THINKING:** If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea.\n\n**YOUR TRY:** Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.\n\n**CHECK:** Read the model and explain where each number or supporting detail came from.","notice":"If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea. Keep your work accurate and name the strategy.","check":{"question":"Main idea?","options":["Bee visits can help pollination","All bees make honey","All flowers need bees","Flowers grow only at night"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct choice is Bee visits can help pollination. If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea."}},{"icon":"✏️","title":"Find supporting details","explain":"**Why this works:** Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail.\n\n**Remember:** Use the original facts, numbers and positions rather than guessing. This lesson investigates main idea detective: find supporting details.","example":"**MY THINKING:** Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail.\n\n**YOUR TRY:** Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.\n\n**CHECK:** Read the model and explain where each number or supporting detail came from.","notice":"Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail. Keep your work accurate and name the strategy.","check":{"question":"Supporting fact?","options":["Pollen may stick to bees","Flowers have wheels","Every hive is enormous","All bees sting"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct choice is Pollen may stick to bees. Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail."}},{"icon":"✅","title":"Check scope and summarise","explain":"**Why this works:** Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion.\n\n**Remember:** Use the original facts, numbers and positions rather than guessing. This lesson investigates main idea detective: find supporting details.","example":"**MY THINKING:** Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion.\n\n**YOUR TRY:** Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.\n\n**CHECK:** Read the model and explain where each number or supporting detail came from.","notice":"Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion. Keep your work accurate and name the strategy.","check":{"question":"How can pollen move?","options":["Bees visit another flower","Pollen walks","A book carries it","Flowers disappear"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct choice is Bees visit another flower. Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion."}}],"worked_example":"Earthworms tunnel through soil, allowing air and water into the ground. They also feed on decaying plant material.\n\n1. Find the topic: A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel.\n\n2. State the main idea: If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea.\n\n3. Find supporting details: Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail.\n\n4. Check scope and summarise: Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion.\n\nCheck how the final answer follows from the original information.","worked_visuals":[{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 590 180\"><rect width=\"590\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6f9f7\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"28\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"18\">Learn, practise and check</text><rect x=\"8\" y=\"60\" width=\"135\" height=\"95\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e0eee4\"/><text x=\"16\" y=\"100\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\">STEP ONE</text><rect x=\"151\" y=\"60\" width=\"135\" height=\"95\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8eefa\"/><text x=\"159\" y=\"100\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\">STEP TWO</text><rect x=\"294\" y=\"60\" width=\"135\" height=\"95\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e0eee4\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"100\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\">STEP THREE</text><rect x=\"437\" y=\"60\" width=\"135\" height=\"95\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8eefa\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"100\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\">STEP FOUR</text></svg>","alt":"Four-stage reminder for Main Idea Detective: Find Supporting Details.","caption":"Use this to keep track of the four teaching ideas."}],"guided_practice":"**First follow the model:** A passage describes earthworms; the topic is earthworms, not just one tunnel. If most sentences describe how worms affect soil, that is the main idea. Worm tunnels allow air and water to enter soil; feeding on old plants is a second supporting detail. Avoid saying all creatures help every plant; the text supports a narrower conclusion.\n\n**Now attempt a new challenge before checking the answer:** Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.\n\n**Feedback:** Topic: native bees and flowers. Main idea: Bee visits can support pollination in some plants. Details: collect nectar; pollen may stick; movement can transfer pollen.\n\n**Important correction:** The passage does not show that every flower requires a bee. A concise summary keeps the main idea and important details without inventing universal claims.","independent_task":"Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.","response_prompt":"Read: ''Native bees visit flowers in many Australian gardens. Some collect nectar, and pollen can stick to their bodies. When bees visit another flower of the same kind, pollen may transfer and help some plants produce seeds.'' Name the topic, one main idea, three details and one claim NOT proven.","quiz":[{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Topic?","options":["Native bees and flowers","Ocean tides","Moon phases","Bicycles"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Native bees and flowers is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Main idea?","options":["Bee visits can help pollination","All bees make honey","All flowers need bees","Flowers grow only at night"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Bee visits can help pollination is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Supporting fact?","options":["Pollen may stick to bees","Flowers have wheels","Every hive is enormous","All bees sting"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Pollen may stick to bees is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"How can pollen move?","options":["Bees visit another flower","Pollen walks","A book carries it","Flowers disappear"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Bees visit another flower is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Unsupported claim?","options":["Pollen may stick","Bees visit flowers","Every flower needs bees","Some plants make seeds"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Every flower needs bees is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Supporting detail means?","options":["Information developing a main idea","An unrelated thought","Only a title","A guess"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Information developing a main idea is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"Topic but not main idea?","options":["Native bees","Bees may carry pollen","Some plants produce seeds","Bees collect nectar"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Native bees is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"multiple_choice","question":"A good short summary includes?","options":["Main idea and key details","Every copied word","An invented villain","Only one adjective"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Main idea and key details is supported by the original worked examples."},{"type":"short_answer","question":"Complete the challenge, showing the supporting information or calculations.","sample_answer":"Topic: native bees and flowers. Main idea: Bee visits can support pollination in some plants. Details: collect nectar; pollen may stick; movement can transfer pollen.","marking_guide":"Check all requested components against the supplied worked method.","explanation":"An answer needs accurate steps, not only a final number."},{"type":"short_answer","question":"Describe and correct a specific possible misconception.","sample_answer":"The passage does not show that every flower requires a bee. A concise summary keeps the main idea and important details without inventing universal claims.","marking_guide":"Require a relevant check and accurate correction.","explanation":"An explanation shows understanding."}],"parent_notes":"Parent: Check the child''s independent strategy or text evidence and ask them to explain one detail without copying.\n\nParent: Written model one: Topic: native bees and flowers. Main idea: Bee visits can support pollination in some plants. Details: collect nectar; pollen may stick; movement can transfer pollen.\n\nParent: Written model two: The passage does not show that every flower requires a bee. A concise summary keeps the main idea and important details without inventing universal claims.\n\nParent: Save original evidence, manually check written work and verify syllabus alignment separately.","evidence_instructions":"Keep the guided practice, original task, one corrected error and the two written quiz responses.","offline_alternative":"Use paper and a pencil for the teaching and independent problem. A parent may record oral explanations.","extension":"Create an additional main idea detective: find supporting details problem and justify its solution.","stage_hints":{"accept":["Read the instruction and find the relevant information for Main Idea Detective: Find Supporting Details.","Think about how the worked example makes each step clear.","Check a specific number or clue, then explain your own answer."],"teac